  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the progression of atherosclerosis in a blood vessel from normal structure to an advanced, rupture-prone lesion. It begins with a naïve vessel showing an open lumen surrounded by the intima and vessel wall. As disease develops, a plaque forms within the wall and protrudes into the lumen, narrowing the channel.\n\nIn the stable plaque stage, the lesion contains macrophages and foam cells beneath a fibrous-looking cap, with vascular smooth muscle cells helping maintain plaque structure. In the next stage, the plaque becomes unstable: the lesion appears larger, the cap thinner or disrupted, and a necrotic core emerges in the center, suggesting cell death and breakdown within the plaque. In the final stage, the unstable plaque is associated with thrombus formation at the luminal surface, indicating rupture or erosion and blood clot development, which further obstructs flow.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes a transition from a healthy vessel to progressively more dangerous plaques characterized by inflammation, lipid-laden foam cells, necrosis, lumen narrowing, and finally thrombosis. The arrow at the bottom highlights this left-to-right disease progression.",
